ingle `xor(off=0,len=168)` | XOR is incremental, so byte-at-a-time absorption must equal one bulk absorb; this is the invariant the shake `_update` chunking relies on. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:431-455 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_xor_bytes` | configuration: `length = 0` at any offset | complete no-op (all three `while` guards are `length > 0` / `length >= 8`). c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:436,442,449 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_xor_bytes` | configuration: XOR the SAME bytes twice at the same offset | must restore the previous state (XOR involution) - a cheap self-check. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:437,443-444 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_extract_bytes` | configuration: `offset` in {0, 1, 7, 8, 71, 135, 167, 199} x `length` in {0, 1, 8, 33, 72, 136, 168} with `offset + length <= 200` | plain `memcpy`, no alignment special-casing (unlike `_xor_bytes`); extraction is non-destructive so repeated extracts at the same offset must agree. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:458-463 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_extract_bytes` | configuration: chunked extraction `(0,32) (32,32) (64,32) (96,32)` vs one `(0,128)` | must concatenate identically; this is the invariant the shake `_squeeze` chunking relies on. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:462 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_permute_24` | configuration: applied to the all-zero state (right after `_init`); then applied N times in a row (N in {1, 2, 3}) | 24 rounds using all of `keccak_round_constants[0..23]`; `permute_24(init)` yields the canonical Keccak-f[1600] zero-state output. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:327-362,391-405 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_permute_12` | configuration: applied to the all-zero state; applied N times (N in {1, 2, 3}); also `permute_12` twice vs `permute_24` once (MUST DIFFER) | 12 rounds using `keccak_round_constants[12..23]` (the TurboSHAKE round set), so 2 x permute_12 is NOT equal to permute_24. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:365-388,408-422 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_permute_24` / `_permute_12` | configuration: state pre-loaded with a known 200-byte pattern via `_xor_bytes(0, 200)`, then permute, then `_extract_bytes(0, 200)` | full-state round-trip; the load/store go through `LOAD64_LE`/`STORE64_LE` so the state is little-endian in memory. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/ref/keccak1600_ref.c:396-404,413-421 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_*` | configuration: \"`_pad`\" — DOES NOT EXIST | padding is open-coded in every caller as `xor_bytes(&domain, offset, 1)` plus `xor_bytes(&0x80, rate-1, 1)`, collapsing to a single `xor_bytes(&(domain ^ 0x80), rate-1, 1)` when `offset == rate - 1`. c_src/libsodium/crypto_xof/shake128/ref/shake128_ref.c:86-96; crypto_hash/sha3/hash_sha3.c:99-107 |\n| `crypto_core_keccak1600_statebytes` | configuration: no arguments | returns 224 (verified at runtime); the permutation only ever touches the first 200 bytes. c_src/libsodium/crypto_core/keccak1600/keccak1600.c:19-23; ref/keccak1600_ref.c:7 |\n| `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305` (one-shot) | configuration: 32-byte key, `inlen` in {0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 1000} | 16 = `poly1305_block_size`; the live implementation is `poly1305_donna32.h` (32x32->64 limbs, `r[5]`/`h[5]` as `unsigned long`), NOT donna64: `HAVE_TI_MODE` is undefined (build flags are only `-Dsodium_EXPORTS -fPIC -std=gnu99`). c_src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna.c:7-11,58-70 |\n| `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305` (one-shot) | configuration: `inlen = 0` with `in = NULL` | valid (verified returns 0): `poly1305_update` does nothing, `poly1305_finish` sees `leftover == 0` and skips the final partial block, so the tag is just `pad` reduced. c_src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna32.h:148-157 |\n| `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305` (one-shot) | configurati