t_enter`, `sodium_crit_leave`, `sodium_version_string`, `sodium_library_version_major/minor`, `sodium_library_minimal`, all 12 `sodium_runtime_has_*`, `randombytes_buf`, `randombytes_buf_deterministic`, `randombytes_random`, `randombytes_uniform`, `randombytes_stir`, `randombytes_close`, `randombytes_set_implementation`, `randombytes_implementation_name`, `randombytes_seedbytes`, `randombytes`, and the exported implementation structs `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation` / `randombytes_internal_implementation`.\n\nGet exact signatures from `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/{utils,core,randombytes,runtime,version}.h`.\n\n## Deliverables\n\nCreate **two** test files:\n* `$ROOT/tests/t10_sodium.rs` — Phase B. One `#[test]` per coherent cluster of CONFIGS G1 rows; each must use MANY randomised inputs (`Rng::new(<fixed seed>)`), not one value. In a doc comment on each test, list the `G1-NNN` config row ids it covers.\n* `$ROOT/tests/t11_sodium_errors.rs` — Phase C. Cover EVERY row of ERRORS.md `## G1`. `return -1` rows are called directly and must match return value + `errno` + every out-parameter + output-buffer contents. `sodium_misuse()`/`assert()` rows use the out-of-process pattern from `HARNESS.md`. In a doc comment on each test, list the `G1-NNN` error row ids it covers.\n\n## Critical correctness notes for this group (verified from source — honour them)\n* `randombytes_set_implementation` is used by the harness; if a test replaces the implementation it MUST restore the harness one afterwards (re-installing `IMPL_C`/`IMPL_R` is not exposed, so instead just call `setup()`-installed impl semantics carefully: prefer testing `randombytes_set_implementation` LAST, in its own `#[test]`, and note that tests run in parallel — put any test that changes global RNG state in a **child process** via the `run_child` mechanism so it cannot disturb other tests).\n* `sodium_mlock`/`munlock`/`mprotect_*` always return `-1` with `errno = ENOSYS` in this build; `sodium_munlock` zeroes the buffer *before* failing.\n* `sodium_stackzero` is an empty body. `sodium_malloc` is plain `malloc(size ? size : 1)`; `sodium_free` is plain `free`; no canary/guard pages. `sodium_free(NULL)` must be a no-op.\n* All 12 `sodium_runtime_has_*` return 0.\n* `sodium_hex2bin`/`sodium_base642bin` can return `-1` while still writing a non-zero `*bin_len`; errno can be overwritten (`ERANGE` -> `EINVAL` when `hex_end`/`b64_end` is NULL); one base64 rejection sets NO errno.\n* `sodium_bin2base64` zero-fills the WHOLE remaining output buffer up to `b64_maxlen`; `sodium_bin2hex` writes only `bin_len*2+1`. Use `canary()` buffers so this is observable.\n* `strchr(ignore, c)` matches the NUL terminator, so any non-NULL `ignore` (even `\"\"`) makes embedded NUL bytes skippable.\n* `sodium_unpad` writes `*unpadded_buflen_p` even when returning `-1`, and never NULL-checks it.\n* Base64 `variant` is an `int`: test all 4 valid variants (1,3,5,7) AND out-of-range values (0,2,4,6,8,-1,`i32::MIN`,`i32::MAX`) across `sodium_base64_encoded_len`, `sodium_bin2base64`, `sodium_base642bin`. This is exactly the out-of-range-enum class the task cares about.\n* `randombytes_uniform` upper_bound 0,1,2,3,large,`0x80000001`; `randombytes_buf_deterministic` size 0/1/63/64/65/1000 with several seeds.\n* `randombytes_sysrandom_implementation.buf(p, 0)` hits an active `assert` -> SIGABRT: reach it by reading the exported struct's `buf` function pointer (the struct is `{name, random, stir, uniform, buf, close}` of 6 pointers, `#[repr(C)]`).\n\n## Method (iterate until green)\n1. Write the Phase B file, run `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test t10_sodium 2>&1 | tail -40`.\n2. Fix failures. **The C is ALWAYS right — never edit anything under `c_src/`.** When C and Rust differ, read the C, then fix the Rust in `src/sodium/*.rs` or `src/randombytes/*.rs`. Re-run until green.\n3. Same for the Phase C file.\n4. Finally run BOTH plus the existing suite: `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features 2>&1 | tail -40` and make sure you broke nothing.\n\n## Rules\n* Only create/modify: `$ROOT/tests/t10_sodium.rs`, `$ROOT/tests/t11_sodium_errors.rs`, and files under `$ROOT/src/sodium/` and `$ROOT/src/randombytes/` (bug fixes only). Do NOT touch `tests/common/mod.rs`, other tests, `Cargo.toml`, or anything in `c_src/`.\n* If a row is genuinely not constructible (needs a multi-GiB buffer, or is UB such as a NULL `nonnull` argument), still write a test that asserts the *nearest reachable* condition, and add a comment naming the row and the reason.\n* Use `timeout` on every command. Keep individual tests under ~60 s.\n\n## Report back\n* The exact paths and line counts of the two files.\n* The number of `#[test]` functions in each.\n* **Every divergence you found between C and Rust, and the exact fix you made** (file + what was wrong). This is the most important part of your report.\n* Which G1 rows you could not construct, and why.\n* Final `cargo test` summary lines."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6415,"cache_read_input_tokens":266075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d07924e0-d479-4dd8-8d3b-8728ce2d0a77","uuid":"20482b53-bef8-4a85-920e-0832b34eb4e6","timestamp":"2026-08-23T08:27:13.915Z"}
