IGS `## G6` row **that `t02_lowlevel.rs` does not already cover**, with MANY randomised inputs per row (`Rng::new(<fixed seed>)`). Doc-comment each `#[test]` with the `G6-NNN` ids it covers. This means at minimum: all `crypto_core_ed25519_*`, all `crypto_core_ristretto255_*`, `crypto_core_ed25519_from_string*` / `_scalar_from_string` (the h2c paths, including the `hash_alg` parameter and `ctx_len > 0xff`), all `crypto_scalarmult_*` (curve25519 / ed25519 / ed25519_noclamp / ristretto255 and their `_base` forms + the generic wrapper), all `crypto_kem_*` (mlkem768 + xwing + generic + `crypto_kem_mlkem768_enc_deterministic`), and all `crypto_ipcrypt_*` (deterministic, `nd`, `ndx`, **and the `pfx` variant**).\n* `$ROOT/tests/t19_core_errors.rs` — Phase C, covering EVERY row of ERRORS `## G6`. Doc-comment each `#[test]` with the `G6-NNN` ids it covers.\n\n## Critical correctness notes (verified from source — honour them)\n* **`softaes.c`: `FAVOR_PERFORMANCE` is NOT defined in this build** (it is auto-defined only for `__wasm__`, and CMake passes no `-D`). So the **1024-entry LUT branch (`softaes.c` ~lines 202-517, `_aes_lut`) is compiled OUT**, and the live round functions are the bitsliced SRM-1R ones (~lines 764/811/849/946). If `src/crypto_core/softaes.rs` translated the LUT half instead, that is a real bug — verify by differentially testing whatever `softaes` reaches through `crypto_ipcrypt_*` and `crypto_aead_aegis*` (softaes has no public exports of its own; drive it through those).\n* **`fe_25_5` is the live ed25519 field representation** (`HAVE_TI_MODE` undefined → `fe25519` is `int32_t[10]`).\n* **ML-KEM decapsulation never returns `-1`** — implicit rejection: a corrupted ciphertext still returns `0` but yields a *different* shared secret. So for `crypto_kem_mlkem768_dec` assert (a) return code equality AND (b) that the C and Rust shared secrets are equal to each other, for both valid and corrupted ciphertexts. `crypto_kem_xwing_dec` can only fail via the **X25519 half** of the ciphertext (`ct[1088..1120]` set to a small-order point).\n* **`crypto_scalarmult_ed25519{,_base}`'s `_is_inf` rejection is unreachable in the clamped forms** but IS reachable in `_noclamp` (`n = L, 2L, … 7L`, or `n = 00..00 80`). Construct those.\n* **`crypto_core_ed25519_add`/`_sub` do NOT validate canonicality / small order / subgroup** — only `frombytes`. Non-canonical and small-order points are accepted there but rejected by `_is_valid_point`. Test both.\n* **`crypto_scalarmult_curve25519`'s all-zero-output check is dead** — every masked small-order x is already in the 7-entry blocklist. Still exercise all 7 blocklisted points plus the all-zero and all-`0xff` inputs.\n* **8 of the 9 `sodium_misuse()` sites in `stream_chacha20.c` are unreachable on x86-64.** The one reachable guard is `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_xor_ic`: `ic + ceil(mlen/64) > 2^32` — smallest case `mlen = 65, ic = 0xffffffff`. Also `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf(clen > 64*(2^32-1))` aborts on the length alone (no buffer touched). Test both out of process. Note that `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_ext_xor_ic` has **no** such guard.\n* **`crypto_ipcrypt_*` entry points are all `void`, with zero rejection branches.** The `pfx`/`ndx` variants have an IPv4-mapped-vs-IPv6 path split and a degenerate-key branch (`k[0..16] == k[16..32]` → re-expand from `k XOR 0x5a`) — exercise both a random key and a degenerate key, and both IPv4-mapped and native IPv6 16-byte inputs.\n* **Absent API** (do not invent): no `crypto_core_ed25519_from_uniform`, no `_from_string_ro`, no `crypto_core_ristretto255_from_string_nu`. Only what `nm -D` shows exists.\n* `crypto_core_h2c` (`core_h2c.c`): an out-of-range `hash_alg` gives `errno = EINVAL; return -1`. `ctx_len > 0xff` is NOT an error — it selects a different DST-derivation path. Test both.\n* Point/scalar shapes to use for ed25519/ristretto255: all-zero, `01 00..00`, `ec ff..ff 7f`, `ff`×32, `00`×31 + `80`, the documented small-order points, `L-1`, `L`, `L+1`, and many random valid points obtained from `crypto_core_*_random` / `_scalarmult_base`.\n\n## Method (iterate until green)\n1. Write the Phase B file; `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test t18_core_scalarmult_kem 2>&1 | tail -40`.\n2. Fix failures. **The C is ALWAYS right — never edit anything under `c_src/`.** On divergence read the C and fix the Rust under `src/crypto_core/`, `src/crypto_scalarmult/`, `src/crypto_stream/`, `src/crypto_kem/`, `src/crypto_ipcrypt/`. Re-run until green.\n3. Same for `--test t19_core_errors`.\n4. Finally `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features 2>&1 | tail -40` to confirm nothing else broke.\n\n## Rules\n* Only create/modify: `$ROOT/tests/t18_core_scalarmult_kem.rs`, `$ROOT/tests/t19_core_errors.rs`, and files under `$ROOT/src/crypto_core/`, `$ROOT/src/crypto_scalarmult/`, `$ROOT/src/crypto_stream/`, `$ROOT/src/crypto_kem/`, `$ROOT/src/crypto_ipcrypt/` (bug fixes only). Do NOT touch `tests/common/mod.rs`, other tests, `Cargo.toml`, or anything in `c_src/`.\n* Use `timeout` on every command; keep individual tests under ~60 s (ML-KEM and ed25519 inversion are not cheap — size the input grids accordingly).\n\n## Report back\n* Exact paths + line counts of the two files, and the number of `#[test]`s in each.\n* **Every C/Rust divergence found and the exact fix made** (file + what was wrong). Most important part — pay special attention to `softaes.rs`.\n* Which G6 rows you could not construct, and why.\n* Final `cargo test` summary lines."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6415,"cache_read_input_tokens":266075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d07924e0-d479-4dd8-8d3b-8728ce2d0a77","uuid":"890925e9-6df1-49e6-9abe-bc40e5c0ecb4","timestamp":"2026-08-23T08:28:15.511Z"}
