.rs` — Phase B gap-filling: any CONFIGS `## G4` row not already covered by `t03_hashes.rs`. Read CONFIGS `## G4` row by row, diff against `t03_hashes.rs`, and write tests for the remainder (with MANY randomised inputs, `Rng::new(<fixed seed>)`). Doc-comment the `G4-NNN` ids covered. If a row is already fully covered by `t03_hashes.rs`, note that in a comment block at the top of the file listing those ids — do not re-test it.\n\n## Critical correctness notes (verified from source — honour them)\n* **There is NO turboshake domain-byte range check.** `_init_with_domain` accepts all 256 values and returns `0`. `DOMAIN_STANDARD` is `0x1F` for all four XOFs.\n* **`crypto_xof_*` has no `_final`, `_absorb` or `_clone`.** Exports per primitive: bare name, `_init`, `_init_with_domain`, `_update`, `_squeeze`, `_statebytes`, `_blockbytes`, `_domain_standard`. Absorb == `_update`; finalisation is implicit in the first `_squeeze`. So **`_update` after `_squeeze`** is the interesting state-machine row — find out exactly what the C does and assert it.\n* **`crypto_core_keccak1600` has no `_absorb`/`_pad`/rate parameter and does ZERO bounds checking** — `offset + length > 200` is silent OOB (UB, not a rejection; note the row).\n* **SHA-3 exports only `sha3256` (rate 136) and `sha3512` (rate 72)** — no 224/384. Domain byte `0x06`. `sha3_update` after `_final` returns `-1` **but still permutes the state and resets `offset`** — a very easy thing to get wrong; test `_final` then `_update` then `_final` and compare both digests.\n* **`crypto_generichash_BYTES_MIN`/`KEYBYTES_MIN` (16) are NOT enforced** — `outlen = 1` and `keylen = 1` both return `0`. Only `1 <= outlen <= 64` and `keylen <= 64` are checked.\n* **`_final` does NOT validate `outlen` against the `outlen` passed to `_init`** — `_init(...,32)` then `_final(...,64)` returns `0`.\n* **Three DIFFERENT outcomes for `crypto_generichash[_blake2b]_final`'s `outlen`:** `outlen == 0` → `sodium_misuse()`/SIGABRT; `outlen` in 65..=255 → `sodium_misuse()`/SIGABRT; `outlen >= 256` → **failed `assert()`** → SIGABRT (different mechanism, different stderr). Meanwhile `_init` and the one-shot return `-1` for those same values. Test all of it; use the out-of-process pattern for the abort rows and remember an `assert` abort does NOT go through the misuse handler (so it dies with signal 6, not exit 77) — assert C and Rust agree on the signal.\n* **Key-NULL asymmetry:** `crypto_generichash(out,32,in,1,NULL,5)` → SIGABRT, but `crypto_generichash_init(state,NULL,5,32)` → returns `0` (silently unkeyed). Test both.\n* **hkdf's only abort path:** `_extract` / `_extract_init` with `salt == NULL && salt_len > 0` → `sodium_misuse()`/SIGABRT (via `crypto_auth_hmacsha*_init`).\n* `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha256_BYTES_MAX = 8160`, `_sha512 = 16320`, both `BYTES_MIN = 0`. `_expand` with `out_len > BYTES_MAX` must reject identically.\n* `crypto_kdf[_blake2b]_derive_from_key` with `subkey_len` below `BYTES_MIN` or above `BYTES_MAX`.\n* poly1305 uses `poly1305_donna32.h` (no `HAVE_TI_MODE`); blake2b uses the ref compress; `crypto_verify_n` uses the portable `volatile uint16_t` loop.\n* Also cover generic-wrapper rejections: `crypto_generichash`, `crypto_hash`, `crypto_shorthash`, `crypto_onetimeauth`, `crypto_kdf` dispatchers.\n\n## Method (iterate until green)\n1. Write `t14_hash_errors.rs`; `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test t14_hash_errors 2>&1 | tail -40`. Fix failures. **The C is ALWAYS right — never edit anything under `c_src/`.**\n2. Write `t15_hash_extra.rs`; `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test t15_hash_extra 2>&1 | tail -40`. Fix failures.\n3. Finally `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features 2>&1 | tail -40` to confirm nothing else broke. Other agents are editing other `src/` subtrees concurrently, so if you see a failure in a test file you did not write and it is not in your subtree, just report it — do not fix it.\n\n## Rules\n* Only create/modify: `$ROOT/tests/t14_hash_errors.rs`