{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PV4GP5qAswsgjAo9JrKtkG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Differential-test harness contract (`tests/common/mod.rs`)\n2\t\n3\tEvery test file starts with:\n4\t\n5\t```rust\n6\tmod common;\n7\tuse common::*;\n8\t```\n9\t\n10\tand calls `setup()` first. **Never call a Rust function directly** — always go\n11\tthrough `dlsym` on the two `.so`s so the `#[no_mangle]` export wrappers are\n12\ttested too.\n13\t\n14\t## Library access\n15\t\n16\t| item | meaning |\n17\t|---|---|\n18\t| `c_lib() -> &'static Library` | the C reference `.so` (`c_src/build/libsodium.so`) |\n19\t| `r_lib() -> &'static Library` | the Rust `.so` (`target/debug/liblibsodium.so`) |\n20\t| `sym::<T>(lib, \"name\") -> T` | look one symbol up, transmuted to fn type `T` |\n21\t| `pair::<T>(\"name\") -> (T, T)` | `(c_fn, rust_fn)` for the same symbol |\n22\t| `has_sym(\"name\") -> bool` | present in both |\n23\t\n24\t`T` is always a bare `unsafe extern \"C\" fn(..) -> ..` type. Map C types as:\n25\t`size_t` -> `usize`, `unsigned long long` -> `u64`, `uint64_t` -> `u64`,\n26\t`uint32_t` -> `u32`, `int` -> `i32`, `unsigned char *` -> `*mut u8`,\n27\t`const unsigned char *` -> `*const u8`, `const char *` -> `*const std::ffi::c_char`,\n28\t`size_t *` -> `*mut usize`, `unsigned long long *` -> `*mut u64`,\n29\tan opaque `*_state *` -> `*mut u8`.\n30\t\n31\t## Setup\n32\t\n33\t* `setup()` — idempotent. Installs a **deterministic `randombytes`\n34\t  implementation into BOTH libraries** (via `randombytes_set_implementation`)\n35\t  and calls `sodium_init()` on both.\n36\t* `reset_rngs(seed)` — rewinds *both* libraries' RNG streams to the same seed.\n37\t  Call it immediately before **each** of the two calls in a pair whose result\n38\t  depends on `randombytes_*` (keypair/keygen/`_random`):\n39\t  ```rust\n40\t  reset_rngs(s); unsafe { c_fn(a.as_mut_ptr()) };\n41\t  reset_rngs(s); unsafe { r_fn(b.as_mut_ptr()) };\n42\t  eq_bytes(\"...\", &a, &b);\n43\t  ```\n44\t\n45\t## Randomised inputs (fixed seed — reproducible)\n46\t\n47\t`Rng::new(seed)` with `.next_u64()`, `.next_u32()`, `.byte()`, `.below(n)`,\n48\t`.range(lo,hi)`, `.fill(&mut [u8])`, `.bytes(n) -> Vec<u8>`, `.bool()`,\n49\t`.pick(&slice)`. Use MANY inputs per row, not one hand-picked value.\n50\t\n51\t## Opaque state buffers\n52\t\n53\t`State::for_sym(\"crypto_x_statebytes\")` allocates a 64-byte-aligned buffer sized\n54\tby the library's own accessor (use this for every `*_state`). `State::new(len)`\n55\tfor an explicit size. `.as_mut_ptr()`, `.as_ptr()`, `.bytes()`, `.len()`.\n56\tAllocate a **separate** state per library — never share one across the two.\n57\t\n58\t`chunks(&mut rng, total, style)` -> `Vec<usize>` summing to `total`;\n59\t`style` 0 = one chunk, 1 = one byte at a time, 2 = two chunks at a random split,\n60\t3 = random walk. Use it to drive `_update` in many different chunkings.\n61\t\n62\t## Comparison\n63\t\n64\t`eq_bytes(what, &c, &r)`, `eq_i32(what, c, r)`, `eq_usize(what, c, r)`,\n65\t`canary(n) -> Vec<u8>` (0xA5-filled output buffer, so writes past the expected\n66\tend are visible), `hex(&[u8]) -> String`.\n67\t\n68\t## `sodium_misuse()` / `abort()` / `assert()` paths — run OUT OF PROCESS\n69\t\n70\t`sodium_misuse()` calls the installed handler and then `abort()`s, so those rows\n71\tmust be run in a child process. Pattern (one `#[test]` child + one `#[test]`\n72\tparent per file):\n73\t\n74\t```rust\n75\tconst MISUSE_CASES: &[&str] = &[\"tag/one\", \"tag/two\"];\n76\t\n77\t#[test]\n78\tfn misuse_child() {\n79\t    let Some((tag, lib)) = child_case() else { return; }; // parent: no-op\n80\t    // `child_case()` already ran setup() and installed the observing handler\n81\t    // on `lib` (the C or the Rust library, per DIFFTEST_WHICH).\n82\t    let mut out = canary(64);\n83\t    match tag.as_str() {\n84\t        \"tag/one\" => {\n85\t            set_observation(out.as_ptr(), out.len()); // printed by the handler\n86\t            let f = sym::<Fn1>(lib, \"crypto_x\");\n87\t            let rc = unsafe { f(/* the misusing arguments */) };\n88\t            println!(\"OBS rc={rc} out={}\", hex(&out));   // only if it returned\n89\t        }\n90\t        _ => panic!(\"unknown tag {tag}\"),\n91\t    }\n92\t    use std::io::Write; let _ = std::io::stdout().flush();\n93\t    std::process::exit(0);           // reached only if the library did NOT abort\n94\t}\n95\t\n96\t#[test]\n97\tfn misuse_paths_match() {\n98\t    if child_tag().is_some() { return; }\n99\t    setup();\n100\t    for &tag in MISUSE_CASES {\n101\t        let c = run_child(\"misuse_child\", \"c\", tag);\n102\t        let r = run_child(\"misuse_child\", \"r\", tag);\n103\t        eq_child(tag, &c, &r);   // same exit code + signal + same \"MISUSE\"/\"OBS\" stdout\n104\t        assert_eq!(c.status.code(), Some(MISUSE_EXIT),\n105\t            \"{tag}: C did not reach sodium_misuse (stderr: {})\",\n106\t            String::from_utf8_lossy(&c.stderr));\n107\t    }\n108\t}\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\t`MISUSE_EXIT == 77`. `set_observation(ptr, len)` registers a byte range that the\n112\thandler prints as `MISUSE obs=<hex>` — use it to compare out-parameters that the\n113\tC writes *before* aborting. For a raw `assert()` failure (no handler involved)\n114\tthe child dies with SIGABRT instead; `eq_child` compares that too, so the same\n115\tpattern works — just don't assert exit code 77 for those, assert\n116\t`c.status.signal() == Some(6)` and that C and Rust agree.\n117\t\n118\t## `errno`\n119\t\n120\t`std::io::Error::last_os_error().raw_os_error()`. Clear it first with a syscall\n121\tknown to succeed (e.g. `let _ = std::fs::metadata(\"/\");`). Common values on\n122\tLinux: `EINVAL=22`, `ERANGE=34`, `ENOMEM=12`, `EFBIG=27`, `ENOSYS=38`,\n123\t`EPERM=1`, `ENXIO=6`.\n124\t\n125\t## Rules\n126\t\n127\t* **Never modify anything in `c_src/`.** The C is ground truth.\n128\t* On any divergence, fix the **Rust** source under `src/`.\n129\t* Build/run with `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test <name>`.\n130\t  (`cargo test` rebuilds the cdylib automatically.)\n131\t* Keep any single test under ~60 s. Skip/downscale genuinely huge work\n132\t  (multi-GiB buffers, `SENSITIVE` pwhash limits) and say so in a comment.\n133\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018AKqc9oZCiiirxxs7Qyssn","session_id":"d07924e0-d479-4dd8-8d3b-8728ce2d0a77","uuid":"87eff50d-f5af-4b97-a821-5a1614ca64da","timestamp":"2026-08-23T08:29:44.605Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Phase B+C tests: box/secretbox/sign/kx/auth"}
