{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_yfxyp4levkydqvxozf5hzwpd6crj6b32laiulo65bougsv6whncq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_016yQjGSSKau1j97kornD5eL","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review secretstream/kx Rust vs C","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"Working dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-rAO86K/translated_rust\n\nThis repo is a Rust translation of libsodium 1.0.23. The C reference is under `c_src/libsodium/`, the Rust port under `src/`.\n\nYour task: read the following C files and their Rust counterparts LINE BY LINE and report EVERY semantic divergence (behaviour, return value, out-parameter writes, order of operations, buffer zeroing, arithmetic, length guards, off-by-one, wrapping).\n\nC files:\n- c_src/libsodium/crypto_secretstream/xchacha20poly1305/secretstream_xchacha20poly1305.c\n- c_src/libsodium/crypto_kx/crypto_kx.c\n\nRust files:\n- src/crypto_secretstream/mod.rs (and any other file under src/crypto_secretstream/)\n- src/crypto_kx/mod.rs (and any other file under src/crypto_kx/)\n\nPay special attention to:\n1. `_pull` writes `*mlen_p = 0` and `*tag_p = 0xff` BEFORE any validation (so observable on every failure path). It does NOT validate the decrypted tag byte.\n2. The message padding formula is literally `(0x10 - (sizeof block) + mlen) & 0xf` where `sizeof block == 64`, which reduces to `mlen & 0xf` (an acknowledged upstream bug). Note this is computed in `unsigned long long` arithmetic — `0x10 - 64 + mlen` wraps. The Rust must reproduce the exact same value. Check for `mlen == 0`: 0x10-64+0 = huge wrapped value, & 0xf = ... compute it: 0x10-64 = -48 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD0; +0 -> 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD0; & 0xf = 0. For mlen=1 -> 0xD1 & 0xf = 1. So yes == mlen & 0xf.\n3. The AD padding is `(0x10 - adlen) & 0xf`.\n4. The poly1305 trailer stores `STORE64_LE(adlen)` then `STORE64_LE(64 + mlen)`.\n5. `_push`: `out[0] = block[0]` is written BEFORE `c = out + 1` is XOR'd (aliasing behaviour matters).\n6. `_pull`: `block[0] = in[0]` twice (once before the xor_ic and once after, restoring the raw byte before the poly1305 update).\n7. rekey condition `(tag & TAG_REKEY) != 0 || sodium_is_zero(counter, 4)` in BOTH push and pull, AFTER `sodium_increment(counter, 4)`.\n8. `_rekey` builds `new_key_and_inonce = k || inonce`, chacha20-ietf-xor with `state->nonce` and `state->k`, then writes back and resets counter to 1.\n9. `_init_push` writes `randombytes_buf(out, 24)` first; state layout is `k[32] || nonce[12] || _pad[8]` where nonce[0..4] is the counter and nonce[4..12] the inonce; `_pad` must be zeroed. statebytes must be 52.\n10. crypto_kx: `rx == NULL` -> `rx = tx`; `tx == NULL` -> `tx = rx`; then `rx == NULL` -> sodium_misuse(). client writes `rx[i]=keys[i]; tx[i]=keys[i+32]` in ONE loop (interleaved per-i!); server writes `tx[i]=keys[i]; rx[i]=keys[i+32]`. The interleaving matters when rx and tx alias. Check the Rust reproduces the exact per-index interleaved write order.\n11. crypto_kx hash input order is always `q || client_pk || server_pk` in BOTH functions.\n12. `crypto_kx_seed_keypair` uses BLAKE2b (crypto_generichash) unkeyed 32-byte output over the 32-byte seed.\n\nReport findings as a concise numbered list: `src/<file>:<line>` — what the Rust does — what the C does. Do NOT edit any file. Be precise and exhaustive; if you find no divergence in a file say so explicitly."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":657,"cache_read_input_tokens":173794,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":657,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018AKqc9oZCiiirxxs7Qyssn","session_id":"d07924e0-d479-4dd8-8d3b-8728ce2d0a77","uuid":"d407fef3-60b6-4f87-88f6-f8ac78c18354","timestamp":"2026-08-23T08:31:48.159Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_descr