{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zmytar5krdy4mukh5wygyjgqzwdrx3x7asnzt6gl2viihryb6gxq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I read all nine C files and all ten Rust files in full, plus the supporting helpers they depend on (`crate::common::{memset,memcpy,memmove}`, `sodium_memzero`, `sodium_memcmp`, `sodium_misuse`, `crypto_verify_n`, `crypto_hash_sha{256,512}_{init,update,final}`, `fe25519_*`, the `_sodium_ge25519_*`/`_sodium_sc25519_*` externs), the C headers for every constant, and I verified the crate builds and emits the expected symbols/constants.\n\n## Result: no semantic divergences found\n\nEvery one of the 9 C files matches its Rust counterpart. Per-file confirmation:\n\n1. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_sign/crypto_sign.c` → `src/crypto_sign/mod.rs` — **no divergence.** All 17 entry points forward to the same ed25519 targets; `crypto_sign_statebytes` = `size_of::<crypto_sign_ed25519ph_state>()`; verified in the built `.so` as `0xd0` = 208. `crypto_sign_PRIMITIVE` is NUL-terminated `b\"ed25519\\0\"`. `crypto_sign_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` compiles to `0xffffffffffffffbf` = `SIZE_MAX - 64`.\n\n2. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_sign/ed25519/sign_ed25519.c` → `src/crypto_sign/ed25519/mod.rs` — **no divergence.** `sk_to_seed`/`sk_to_pk` use `core::ptr::copy` with correct (src, dst) order (`copy(sk, seed, 32)` and `copy(sk+32, pk, 32)`). `ph_init` discards the sha512_init return and returns 0; `ph_update` propagates the return value (matching C). `_final_create`/`_final_verify` call `crypto_hash_sha512_final` (the Rust one, which does `sodium_memzero(state, 208)` — item 12 preserved) into a 64-byte `ph`, then delegate with `prehashed = 1` and `mlen = 64`. `crypto_sign_ed25519ph_statebytes` = 208 (confirmed `0xd0`).\n\n3. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/keypair.c` → `src/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/keypair.rs` — **no divergence.** `seed_keypair` writes the full 64-byte SHA-512 of the seed into `sk`, clamps `sk[0] &= 248; sk[31] &= 127; sk[31] |= 64`, scalarmult_base over `sk`, `p3_tobytes(pk)`, then `copy(seed → sk, 32)` and `copy(pk → sk+32, 32)`, giving `sk = seed || pk` (item 4 exact). `pk_to_curve25519` has exactly the three OR'd `return -1` conditions (`frombytes_negate_vartime != 0`, `has_small_order != 0`, `is_on_main_subgroup == 0`) and no `ge25519_is_canonical` call (item 5 exact); the fe25519 sequence `1; 1-Y; 1; 1+Y; invert; mul; tobytes` is preserved, with `fe25519_invert_in_place` correctly modelling `fe25519_invert(z, z)`. `sk_to_curve25519` clamps and copies only 32 of the 64 bytes, then zeroes all 64.\n\n4. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/open.c` → `src/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/open.rs` — **no divergence.** `src/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/open.rs:70-74` implements exactly `if ((sig[63] & 240) != 0 && sc25519_is_canonical(sig+32) == 0) return -1;` followed by `if (ge25519_is_canonical(pk) == 0) return -1;` — the `sig[63] & 224` `ED25519_COMPAT` branch is correctly absent (item 1). `ACQUIRE_FENCE` → no-op, correct for a build with no `HAVE_*` macros. Return value is `has_small_order(&check) - 1`. `crypto_sign_ed25519_open` (`open.rs:161-188`): the `smlen < 64 || smlen-64 > MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` path jumps straight to `badsig` and only sets `*mlen_p = 0` with **no** `memset` of `m`; the verify-failure path does `memset(m, 0, mlen)` guarded by `m != NULL` and *then* `*mlen_p = 0`; both return -1 (item 2 exact). `*mlen_p` is written before the `memmove(m, sm+64, mlen)` on success, as in C.\n\n5. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/sign.c` → `src/crypto_sign/ed25519/ref10/sign.rs` — **no divergence.** DOM2PREFIX is byte-for-byte the 34-byte `\"SigEd25519 no Ed25519 collisions\"` + `0x01 0x00` (item 7). `_crypto_sign_ed25519_detached` reproduces the exact 16-step order from item 6, including `memmove(sig+32, sk+32, 32)` *before* `sc25519_reduce(nonce)` (so `sig[32..64]` transiently holds the pk and is hashed as part of `update(sig, 64)`), the second `hinit(prehashed)`, `sc25519_reduce(hram)`, `clamp(az)`, `sc25519_muladd(sig+32, hram, az, nonce)`, then `memzero(az)`, `memzero(nonce)` (hram deliberately not zeroed, as in C), `*siglen_p = 64` only when non-NULL, return 0. No `randombytes` call — the `ED25519_NONDETERMINISTIC` branch is correctly absent (item 1). `crypto_sign_ed25519` does `memmove(sm+64, m, mlen)` first and signs over `sm+64`, not `m` (item 3 exact); the unreachable error path zeroes `mlen + 64` bytes with `wrapping_add`, matching C's unsigned-long-long wrap, and `*smlen_p = mlen + siglen` likewise wraps.\n\n6. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_auth/crypto_auth.c` → `src/crypto_auth/mod.rs` — **no divergence.** `crypto_auth_BYTES` = 32, `KEYBYTES` = 32, primitive `b\"hmacsha512256\\0\"`, `crypto_auth`/`crypto_auth_verify` forward to hmacsha512256, `keygen` fills 32 bytes.\n\n7. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_auth/hmacsha256/auth_hmacsha256.c` → `src/crypto_auth/hmacsha256.rs` — **no divergence.** `hmacsha256.rs:88-98` is the exact `if keylen > 64 { hash into khash; key = khash; keylen = 32 } else if key.is_null() { if keylen > 0 { sodium_misuse() } }` structure. Item 8 is preserved: with `key == NULL, keylen > 64` the first branch is taken and `crypto_hash_sha256_update(ictx, NULL, keylen)` is reached; the Rust `crypto_hash_sha256_update` passes its `inlen == 0` early-out, then dereferences `*in_.add(i)` on a null pointer → SIGSEGV, not a clean `sodium_misuse`/SIGABRT. Pads 0x36 then 0x5c, 64 bytes each, both XOR loops bounded by the same `keylen`, `memzero(pad, 64)` then `memzero(khash, 32)`, return 0. `_final` order (final ictx → ihash, update octx with 32, final octx → out, memzero ihash) is identical, and both `_final`s go through the state-zeroing `crypto_hash_sha256_final` (item 12). `_verify` is `crypto_verify_32(h, correct) | (-(h==correct)) | sodium_memcmp(correct, h, 32)` with the same operand order (item 11). `statebytes` = 208 (`0xd0` in the `.so`).\n\n8. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_auth/hmacsha512/auth_hmacsha512.c` → `src/crypto_auth/hmacsha512.rs` — **no divergence.** Threshold is `> 128` (not 64), `khash[64]`, `keylen = 64`, `pad[128]` filled with 0x36/0x5c, updates of 128 bytes, `crypto_verify_64`, `sodium_memcmp(..., 64)`, `statebytes` = 416 (`0x1a0`) — item 9 satisfied.\n\n9. `c_src/libsodium/crypto_auth/hmacsha512256/auth_hmacsha512256.c` → `src/crypto_auth/hmacsha512256.rs` — **no divergence.** Pure forwarder; `crypto_auth_hmacsha512256_state` is a `type` alias of `crypto_auth_hmacsha512_state` (same typedef relationship, `statebytes` = 416, confirmed `0x1a0`); truncation to 32 bytes happens only in `_final`, followed by `memzero(out0, 64)`; `_verify` uses `crypto_verify_32` / `sodium_memcmp(..., 32)` (items 10 and 13 satisfied).\n\n## Differences that are intentional and non-semantic (verified harmless)\n\n- Rust zero-initializes locals that C leaves uninitialized (`hs`, `az`/`nonce`/`hram`, `Ge25519P3::default()`, `state` in the one-shot `crypto_auth_*` wrappers). In every case the buffer is fully written before any read, so no observable difference.\n- `crate::common::memset`/`memcpy`/`memmove` skip the call when `n == 0`; C's `memset`/`memcpy` with `n == 0` are no-ops, so behaviour is identical.\n- `hmacsha256.rs` / `hmacsha512.rs` each declare a private copy of `crypto_hash_sha{256,512}_state`; both are `#[repr(C)]` with layouts identical to `src/crypto_hash/sha{256,512}.rs` (104 and 208 bytes), so the FFI calls and `statebytes` agree with C.\n- `pad[i] ^= *key.add(i)` uses bounds-checked indexing, but after the `keylen > 64` / `keylen > 128` normalization `keylen` can never exceed the pad length, so the check can never fire.\n- `ACQUIRE_FENCE` is correctly modelled as nothing (`(void) 0`) for the \"no `HAVE_*` macros\" build described in `CONFIGS.md`.\n- `sodium_misuse()` is typed `-> !` in Rust and still `abort()`s after running the handler, matching `core.c:192-205`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"inpu